\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $\\mathbf T$ is $[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since $\\det(A) > 0,$ the affine transformation $\\mathbf T$ is orientation-preserving.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139534696 true] [17592186179363 1100 "Consider the affine transformation $\\mathbf T$, defined as\n\n$x = 3u+4, \\qquad y = 4v - 2u$.\n\nWhat is the area scale factor of the transformation?" 13194139534696 false] [17592186179363 1100 "Consider the affine transformation $\\mathbf T,$ defined as\n\n$$\nx = 3u+4, \\qquad y = 4v - 2u.\n$$\n\nWhat is the area scale factor of the transformation?" 13194139534696 true] [17592186179363 1145 "An affine transformation $\\mathbf T:\\mathbb R^2\\rightarrow\\mathbb R^2$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\n\\mathbf x = A \\mathbf u + \\mathbf b\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf x, \\mathbf u$, and $\\mathbf b$ are column vectors in $\\mathbb R^2$, and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nNotice that the given transformation can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}3u + 4 \\\\ 4v - 2u\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}3u \\\\ -2u + 4v\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}_(⏟)_(A) \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}_(⏟)_(u) + \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}_(⏟)_(b)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $\\mathbf T$